{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdfmgFJ4cphG86w4o8kq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011n9ePfNYyQnXUQFgFqmiVw","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PYEOF'\nentry='''\n## 2026-11-02: wiki toolbar syntax moved to JavaScript (#43259)\n\nThe wiki toolbar no longer renders the syntax of each button. Redmine::WikiToolbar renders the format name (data-wiki-toolbar-format-value) and the button names, grouped; the text formatters (CommonMark and Textile) only give those. The wiki-toolbar Stimulus controller defines what each button inserts, per format, in its FORMATS table. Normal buttons use wiki-toolbar#apply with a button param, precode languages use wiki-toolbar#insertPrecode with a lang param, and keyboard shortcuts use one generic keydown action. Only the strong, em and ins buttons carry a shortcut attribute, and the keyboard actions are the generic one plus the two Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+P preview toggles.\n\nWhat was learned: Stimulus key filters require every modifier to match exactly, so the generic shortcut handler must reject Shift and Alt, or Ctrl+Shift+B would apply bold. The helper memoizes the toolbar per view, so a test that renders two formats must use a fresh view for each one.\n\nWhat is left: there is no JavaScript unit test runner in this repository, so the browser behavior is covered by the system tests in test/system/wiki_toolbar_test.rb and test/system/keyboard_shortcuts_test.rb, which need Chrome. The precode language param passes through Stimulus param typecasting, so a language name that looks like a number (for example 010) would not come back unchanged; the old markup had the same kind of typecasting risk, and no language in the defaults is affected.\n'''\np='docs/harness/log.md'\ns=open(p).read()\nif not s.endswith('\\n'): s+='\\n'\nopen(p,'w').write(s+entry)\nPYEOF\ntail -5 docs/harness/log.md | cut -c1-120","description":"Append dated entry to harness implementation log"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":663,"cache_read_input_tokens":113796,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":663},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2425fc2-2305-46c0-849a-8faf55fe3ddb","uuid":"19d0886f-d788-43fe-817f-3a2ffdccb5aa","timestamp":"2026-11-02T06:59:48.674Z","request_id":"req_011CgdfmfwwH5afnXwoh4Vi7","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_011n9ePfNYyQnXUQFgFqmiVw":{"command":"cd /Users/victor/Repos/unwritten/uw-harness-cases/redmine/bench/trees/redmine-1-harness-haiku && python3 - <<'PYEOF'\nentry='''\n## 2026-11-02: wiki toolbar syntax moved to JavaScript (#43259)\n\nThe wiki toolbar no longer renders the syntax of each button. Redmine::WikiToolbar renders the format name (data-wiki-toolbar-format-value) and the button names, grouped; the text formatters (CommonMark and Textile) only give those. The wiki-toolbar Stimulus controller defines what each button inserts, per format, in its FORMATS table. Normal buttons use wiki-toolbar#apply with a button param, precode languages use wiki-toolbar#insertPrecode with a lang param, and keyboard shortcuts use one generic keydown action. Only the strong, em and ins buttons carry a shortcut attribute, and the keyboard actions are the generic one plus the two Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+P preview toggles.\n\nWhat was learned: Stimulus key filters require every modifier to match exactly, so the generic shortcut handler must reject Shift and Alt, or Ctrl+Shift+B would apply bold. The helper memoizes the toolbar per view, so a test that renders two formats must use a fresh view for each one.\n\nWhat is left: there is no JavaScript unit test runner in this repository, so the browser behavior is covered by the system tests in test/system/wiki_toolbar_test.rb and test/system/keyboard_shortcuts_test.rb, which need Chrome. The precode language param passes through Stimulus param typecasting, so a language name that looks like a number (for example 010) would not come back unchanged; the old markup had the same kind of typecasting risk, and no language in the defaults is affected.\n'''\np='docs/harness/log.md'\ns=open(p).read()\nif not s.endswith('\\n'): s+='\\n'\nopen(p,'w').write(s+entry)\nPYEOF\ntail -5 docs/harness/log.md | cut -c1-120","description":"Append dated entry to harness implementation log"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_011n9ePfNYyQnXUQFgFqmiVw":{"cwd":"/Users/victor/Repos/unwritten/uw-harness-cases/redmine/bench/trees/redmine-1-harness-haiku"}}}
